# nr-office-comrs-dispatch: the late-bound COM layer of mixdog-office-com (src/com/). Applied by the wave's wiring.

The files are written under native/core/crates/mixdog-office-com/. The three edits below are in files this task does not own
(the crate's lib.rs and Cargo.toml belong to nr-office-com-transport / nr-office-comrs-transport-host).

## 1. native/core/crates/mixdog-office-com/src/lib.rs
After the line `mod cleanup;` add:
    pub mod com;
(`com` is public: the Office ports of the later tasks and the tests in tests/ use it. On non-Windows targets the module
compiles without its `live` backend; `fake` is compiled for `cfg(any(test, feature = "test-support"))`.)

## 2. native/core/crates/mixdog-office-com/Cargo.toml
The crate sets `autotests = false`, so the two new test files are listed. After the `[[test]] name = "twin_detect"` block add:
    [[test]]
    name = "dispatch"
    path = "tests/dispatch.rs"

    [[test]]
    name = "dispatch_live"
    path = "tests/dispatch_live.rs"

No dependency line is added: the layer calls oleaut32, ole32 and kernel32 through its own `extern "system"` declarations
(src/com/live.rs), so it needs no `windows` crate feature, no new edge to mixdog-workspace-hack and no Cargo.lock change.
The tests use serde_json, sha2 and the crate itself with feature `test-support`, all already in [dependencies] and
[dev-dependencies]. (The plan text names the `windows` crate for this layer; that was written before the raw-ABI form was
chosen. If the wiring prefers the `windows` crate, only live.rs changes, not the API.)

## 3. native/core/crates/mixdog-office-com/Cargo.toml: nothing else; mixdog-office-com/tests/dispatch.rs and dispatch_live.rs
dispatch_live.rs is `#![cfg(windows)]` and both of its tests are `#[ignore]` (they start Word and Excel).

## 4. The pause between retries
com::retry takes a `&dyn com::retry::Pause` (one method, `pause(Duration)`). The platform layer's Clock
(nr-office-comrs-platform, `sleep(ms)` as a pumped wait) implements it:
    impl mixdog_office_com::com::retry::Pause for <the platform clock> { fn pause(&self, d: Duration) { self.sleep(d.as_millis() as u64) } }
Tests use com::fake::ManualClock, which records the pauses and never waits.

## 5. Interface for the ports (nr-office-comrs-*)
    com::Obj: get, get_with, put, put_with, put_ref, call, call_named, enumerate, items, count, item, same_object, identity_key
    com::Value (Empty, Null, Bool, I1..UI8, R4, R8, Currency, Date, Str, Error, Decimal, Dispatch, Unknown, Array, Missing),
      Value::member / call_member / set_member (the null-receiver rules), as_obj / as_str / as_bool / as_i64 / as_f64
    com::SafeArray (one_d, from_rows, zero_based, get, rows, dims), com::Error (Com | Host), com::retry::{excel, powerpoint}
    com::live (Windows): create_object (New-Object -ComObject), active_object (GetActiveObject), Sta (apartment guard),
      LiveObject::from_dispatch, system_text
    com::calllog (the recording format: JSON lines; the recorder writes it with entry_line/encode_value), com::fake::Fake
No protocol method, no SERVICE_PENDING name, no hot-file edit is involved.

## 6. tests/fixtures/errors/corpus.json and MANIFEST.json
Both hold hand-written seed entries (`"recorded": false`) until recorder/errors-corpus.ps1 is run in Windows PowerShell 5.1
with Microsoft Office on the migration PC; the script rewrites both files (`"recorded": true`, PowerShell version, Office
builds, sha256). tests/dispatch.rs holds errtext.rs to whichever corpus is there, and dispatch_live_errors needs the recorded
entries (its ids are the recorder's).
